tel 1.0

base acyclicity
import soundness

info   directed graphs as values: acyclic by construction, ordered, searched, edited and drawn
detail
    _Acyclicity_ represents a directed graph — the shape of a build system, a task schedule, a
    type hierarchy or a package's dependencies — as an immutable value with the operations such
    graphs need: a topological order, reachability, transitive closure and reduction, slicing
    around a node, and editing that keeps the graph acyclic. Its central type is `Dag`, a graph
    which **cannot hold a cycle**: every way of making one either checks once (and raises
    `Dag.Error`) or preserves acyclicity by construction, so `linearized`, `reduction` and
    `traversal` have no failure mode. Beside it sit `Digraph`, the general directed graph which
    may contain a cycle and is converted to a `Dag` by the one checked step, `acyclic`; and
    `Topology`, a graph edited in place which keeps its topological order live as edges arrive,
    and which, frozen, is the form laid out for fast querying.

    Every edge is read as a **dependency**: `a -> b` means `a` depends on `b`, so a node's
    `successors` are its dependencies, its `predecessors` are its dependants, the `sources` are
    the nodes depending on nothing (where a build starts) and the `sinks` the nodes nothing
    depends on. A topological order lists every node *after* its dependencies.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val dag = Dag(8 -> Set(4, 6), 6 -> Set(3, 2), 4 -> Set(2), 3 -> Set(), 2 -> Set())

    dag.linearized       // List(2, 4, 3, 6, 8): dependencies before dependents
    dag.reachable(8)     // Set(8, 4, 6, 2, 3)
    dag.sources          // Set(2, 3)
    dag.add(8, 3)        // a new Dag; raises Dag.Error if the edge would close a cycle
    dag.reduction        // only the edges no path already implies
    ```

    All of these, and anything else graph-shaped — a `Hasse` diagram, a `Map[node, Set[node]]`,
    a `Ledger` — share one vocabulary through the `Nodal` typeclass (a node set and each node's
    successors), with `Bidirectional` for a representation which also stores predecessors,
    `Topological` for one whose values can hold no cycle, and `Reachable` and `Invertible` for
    those with a native search or inversion. The package-level extensions (`nodes`, `edges`,
    `sources`, `sinks`, `cycle`, `acyclic`, `reachable`, `closure`, `invert`, and for
    `Topological` graphs `linearized`, `reduction`, `traversal` and `layered`) are written once
    against those typeclasses, so a `Hasse` or a `Map` answers them exactly as a `Dag` does.

    A `Dag` or `Digraph` stores only the forward direction, so locating a node's predecessors is
    a scan of every edge. The operations that need it — `predecessors`, `ancestors`, `lineage`,
    `bypass`, `bypassAll` and `-` — are gated on `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`, acknowledged with
    `import dysasymptotics.linearScan`; `subgraph`, which rebuilds the whole map for a bounded
    choice of nodes, is gated on `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize` (`import dysasymptotics.linearSize`).
    A frozen `Topology` keeps both directions and needs neither.

    A `Dag` draws as a GraphViz DOT document through `Dot`, with `dag.dot` on a `Dag[Text]`; the
    `dendrology` library draws any `Topological` graph in the terminal, and `Dag#layered`
    arranges one in crossing-minimised layers for it.

    The library is one source set, `acyclicity.core`, compiled for every platform and exported
    to the `soundness` umbrella, where the `rankings` choice package merges with the umbrella's
    own. The only given a user imports by name is `rankings.balancedRanking`; everything else
    resolves from the companions.

refer acyclicity.Dag
refer acyclicity.Digraph
refer acyclicity.Topology
refer acyclicity.Nodal
refer acyclicity.Hasse
refer acyclicity.Dot

entry .Dag   a directed acyclic graph as a persistent value, with a topological order that cannot fail
  detail
      A `Dag[node]` is a finite directed graph which holds no cycle, as an immutable value: an
      insertion-ordered map from each node to the set of nodes it points at — under the
      dependency reading, its dependencies. Every edge target is itself a node, so a dangling
      edge is unrepresentable. The absence of a cycle is established once, when the value is
      made: the factories `Dag(…)` check (raising `Dag.Error` with `Reason.Cyclic`), `acyclic`
      converts a `Digraph` with the same check, and `Dag(topology)` consumes a `Topology`, which
      is acyclic by invariant. Every edit then either cannot create a cycle (`-`, `remove`,
      `bypass`, `subgraph`, `descendants`), checks the one path that could (`add`), or answers a
      `Digraph` where a cycle is possible (`+`, `++`, `map`, `flatMap`).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val diamond = Dag(t"a" -> t"b", t"a" -> t"c", t"b" -> t"d", t"c" -> t"d")

      diamond.linearized          // List(d, b, c, a): every node after its dependencies
      diamond.sources             // Set(d): depending on nothing
      diamond.sinks               // Set(a): nothing depends on it
      diamond.reachable(t"b")     // Set(b, d)
      diamond.add(t"d", t"a")     // raises Dag.Error: `a` already reaches `d`
      ```

      What acyclicity buys is `linearized`, `reduction` and `traversal` without a failure mode,
      and the `Topological` instance which lets the generic extensions have them too. As a
      collection, a `Dag` is its nodes in topological order (`traversable`), so `dag.has(n)`,
      `dag.size`, `dag.each(…)` and `dag.map(…)` apply; `map` answers a `Digraph`, since two
      nodes may be renamed to one and close a cycle.

      A `Dag` can be built one edge at a time:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The first edge: both ends become nodes
        code
            val build = Dag(t"app" -> t"lib")
            build.linearized      // List(lib, app)
      frame
        label A second dependency, checked as it is added
        code
            val build = Dag(t"app" -> t"lib").add(t"lib", t"core")
            build.linearized      // List(core, lib, app)
      frame
        label An edge against the order, refused
        code
            val build = Dag(t"app" -> t"lib").add(t"lib", t"core")
            safely(build.add(t"core", t"app"))   // Unset: `app` already reaches `core`
      frame
        label An edge which may close a cycle answers a Digraph instead
        code
            val build = Dag(t"app" -> t"lib").add(t"lib", t"core")
            (build + (t"core" -> t"app")).cycle   // List(core, app, lib, core)
      ##
      ```

      Each checked `add` costs the reach of the new edge's target, so a graph of many edges is
      built with one `Dag(edges*)` call (one O(n + e) check) rather than a fold of `add`s, and a
      graph edited many times is better thawed into a `Topology`, edited in place, and frozen
      again. Whole-graph queries which depend on reachability — `closure`, `reduction` — go
      through the frozen form's bit matrix, so a graph queried that way more than once is best
      frozen once with `#freeze` and kept.

      Two `Dag`s are equal when their adjacency is, whatever order their nodes were given in;
      a `Dag` is not a `case class`, so it has no `unapply` or `copy`.
  refer acyclicity.Digraph
  refer acyclicity.Topology
  refer acyclicity.Nodal
  refer acyclicity.Layering
  entry .apply   a `Dag` from edges, from nodes with their successors, from a node set and a function, from a `Map`, or from a `Topology`
      Five constructors, four of which check for a cycle and raise `Dag.Error` (`Reason.Cyclic`)
      if they find one:

      - `Dag(a -> b, a -> c, …)` from edges alone: both ends of every edge become nodes, in order
        of first appearance.
      - `Dag(a -> Set(b, c), b -> Set(), …)` from each node with its dependency set; a successor
        given no entry of its own becomes a node with no dependencies.
      - `Dag(nodes)(node => dependencies)` from a node set and a function; a successor outside
        the set becomes a node. This one takes its `Tactic[Dag.Error]` as an ordinary contextual
        parameter rather than a `raises` clause.
      - `Dag(adjacency)` from a `Map[node, Set[node]]`, closing the node set over every target.
      - `Dag(topology)` from a `Topology[node]^`, which is **consumed**: the topology is acyclic by
        invariant, so there is no check and no `Dag.Error`, and the handle cannot be used again.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Dag(8 -> 4, 8 -> 6, 6 -> 3)
      Dag(8 -> Set(4, 6), 6 -> Set(3, 2), 4 -> Set(2))
      Dag(Set(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12))(n => Set.from((1 until n).filter(n%_ == 0)))
      Dag[Text]()                      // the empty graph
      ```

      `Dag(1 -> 2, 2 -> 3, 3 -> 1)` raises `Dag.Error`; `Digraph(…)` with the same arguments
      makes the cyclic graph, and `cycle` on it is the witness.
  entry .dot   a `Dag[Text]` as a GraphViz DOT digraph, each node's text as its identifier
      An extension on `Dag[Text]`: one `Dot.Digraph` with an edge statement `"a" -> "b"` for
      every edge. A node's text names its DOT node, so it must be a valid DOT identifier —
      non-empty, with no double-quote and no newline — and a node which is not raises
      `Name.Error`; a `Tactic[Name.Error]` must be in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Dag(t"a" -> t"b").dot.serialize   // "\ndigraph {\n  \"a\" -> \"b\"\n}"
      ```

      A graph of other nodes renders through `map(_.show).acyclic.dot` (the `map` answers a
      `Digraph`, since two nodes could show as one).
  entry .Error   raised when a graph operation finds a cycle or is asked about a node the graph lacks
    detail
        Error 191, _the DAG operation failed because `reason`_, with the reason numbered within
        it. `Reason.Cyclic` (191.2, _the graph contains a cycle_) is raised by the checking
        factories `Dag(…)`, by `acyclic` on any graph, by `Dag#add` for an edge whose target
        already reaches its source, and by `Topology#add` likewise; the graph being edited is left
        unchanged. `Reason.NodeMissing(node)` (191.1, _the node `node` is not present in the
        graph_) is raised by `reachable`, `descendants`, `ancestors` and `lineage` for a node the
        graph does not have — on a `Dag`, a `Digraph` and through the generic extension alike —
        with the node rendered by its `toString`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val cyclic = safely(Dag(1 -> 2, 2 -> 1))        // Unset
        val missing = capture[Dag.Error](unsafely(Dag(1 -> 2)).reachable(9)).reason
        // Dag.Error.Reason.NodeMissing(t"9")
        ```
    entry .Reason   why the operation failed: `Cyclic`, or `NodeMissing(node)`
        An enum with `number` (1 for `NodeMissing`, 2 for `Cyclic`), each case a
        `Clarification` which the error's message quotes.
    entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message
    entry #reason   the `Dag.Error.Reason`: `Cyclic` or `NodeMissing(node)`
  entry .nodal   the `Nodal` instance: a `Dag`'s nodes and each node's successors
      In implicit scope through the companion. `nodes` are the nodes in the order they were
      given, `successors(n)` the nodes `n` points at (empty for a node the graph lacks), and
      `has` is a map lookup, never a scan.
  entry .bidirectional   the `Bidirectional` instance, gated on `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`
      A `Dag` stores only the forward direction, so its predecessors come from a transpose
      computed once per value by scanning every edge. The instance therefore takes a
      `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`, acknowledged with `import dysasymptotics.linearScan`, which is
      what the generic `predecessors` extension needs on a `Dag`. A frozen `Topology` answers
      the same question without the gate.
  entry .topological   the marker which certifies a `Dag` holds no cycle
      The `Topological` instance, in implicit scope. It is what admits a `Dag` to `linearized`,
      `reduction`, `traversal` and `layered` through the generic extensions, and to the
      `dendrology` diagrams.
  entry .traversable   a `Dag` as a collection of its nodes, in topological order
      The `Traversable` instance, so `dag.each`, `dag.fold`, `dag.to[List]` and the rest of the
      collection vocabulary see the nodes with every node after its dependencies.
  entry .inclusive   `dag.has(node)`: whether the node is in the graph
  entry .countable   `dag.size` and `dag.nil`: how many nodes, and whether there are none
  entry .mappable   `dag.map(lambda)`: renames the nodes, answering a `Digraph`
      The `Mappable` instance, with `Result[node2] = Digraph[node2]`: two nodes may map to one,
      which unites their edges and may close a cycle, so the result is not a `Dag` until
      `acyclic` checks it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Dag(1 -> 3, 2 -> 4).map(n => if n < 3 then t"lo" else t"hi").edges   // Set((lo, hi))
      Dag(1 -> 2).map(_.show).acyclic                                       // a Dag[Text]
      ```
  entry #nodes   every node, as a set
  entry #successors   the nodes a node points at: its dependencies, or the empty set for an absent node
  entry #edges   every edge as a `(from, to)` pair: `from` depends on `to`
  entry #sources   the nodes with no successors: those depending on nothing
      Where a build or an installation starts. Whole-graph in scope, so no acknowledgement is
      needed.
  entry #sinks   the nodes with no predecessors: those nothing depends on
      One pass over the edges, with no `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan` needed: the question is about
      the whole graph, not one node.
  entry #linearized   the nodes in topological order: every node after everything it depends on
      Computed once per value and kept. Between nodes with no path between them, the order is
      the order the nodes were given, so it is stable. It cannot fail: a `Dag` has no cycle.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Dag(8 -> Set(4, 6), 6 -> Set(3, 2), 4 -> Set(2)).linearized   // List(2, 4, 3, 6, 8)
      ```
  entry #invert   the same graph with every edge reversed, still a `Dag`
      Reversing every edge preserves acyclicity, so the result is a `Dag` whose sources are this
      graph's sinks; `invert.invert` is equal to the original.
  entry #digraph   the same graph as a `Digraph`, for the operations which may create a cycle
  entry #freeze   the frozen `Topology[node]^{}` of this graph, laid out for querying
      Builds a `Topology` with the nodes already in topological order and freezes it. The frozen
      form keeps both directions of adjacency, so `predecessors` needs no acknowledgement, and
      answers `reaches` in O(1) once its reachability matrix is built; `closure` and `reduction`
      on a `Dag` go through it. A graph queried this way repeatedly should be frozen once.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val frozen: Topology[Int]^{} = Dag(8 -> 4, 4 -> 2).freeze
      frozen.reaches(8, 2)        // true
      frozen.predecessors(2)      // Set(4)
      frozen.snapshot             // back to the Dag
      ```
  entry #thaw   an editable `Topology[node]^` holding this graph's nodes and edges
      The same as `Topology(dag)`: a fresh, exclusively-owned topology, edited in place with
      `add`, `remove` and `bypass`, and given back as a `Dag` with `Dag(topology)` or
      `Topology.freeze`. The right form for many edits, which cost about a microsecond each
      there against the reach-sized check of `Dag#add`.
  entry #including   the graph with a node added, with no edges, if it was absent
  entry #add   the graph with the edge `from -> to` added; raises `Dag.Error` if it would close a cycle
      `from` depends on `to`. The one way an edge can close a cycle is for `to` already to reach
      `from` (or `from == to`), which is checked by a search of `to`'s reach — so the cost is the
      size of that reach, not of the graph. A new target becomes a node. The result is a `Dag`;
      `+` is the unchecked form, answering a `Digraph`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val dag = Dag(t"a" -> t"b")
      dag.add(t"b", t"c").linearized       // List(c, b, a)
      safely(dag.add(t"b", t"a"))          // Unset: refused
      ```
  entry #+   `dag + (from -> to)`: the edge added without a check, as a `Digraph`
      The result may hold a cycle, so it is a `Digraph`; `acyclic` checks it back into a `Dag`,
      and `cycle` shows the witness if there is one. `add` is the checked form.
  entry #++   `dag ++ other`: the union of two graphs' nodes and edges, as a `Digraph`
      Two acyclic graphs can unite into a cyclic one, so the result is a `Digraph`.
  entry #remove   the graph with the edge `from -> to` removed, both nodes kept
      Unchanged if the edge was not there. Removing an edge cannot create a cycle, so the
      result is a `Dag`.
  entry #-   `dag - node`: the node and every edge at either end of it dropped
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan` (`import dysasymptotics.linearScan`), since the edges
      pointing at the node are found through the transpose. The dependants of the node lose
      that dependency and gain no other; `bypass` is the form which reroutes them.
  entry #bypass   the node dropped, with each of its dependants rerouted to each of its dependencies
      Every path through the node survives: each predecessor gains every successor as a direct
      dependency. This cannot create a cycle, since each predecessor already reached each
      successor through the node. Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dysasymptotics.linearScan

      val diamond = Dag(t"a" -> t"b", t"a" -> t"c", t"b" -> t"d", t"c" -> t"d")
      diamond.bypass(t"b").successors(t"a")   // Set(c, d)
      (diamond - t"b").successors(t"a")       // Set(c)
      ```
  entry #bypassAll   several nodes bypassed one after another, so a path through a run of them survives
      Takes a `Set` of nodes rather than a predicate — a lambda argument could not be told from
      a node — and bypasses them in turn, so that `Dag(1 -> 2, 2 -> 3, 3 -> 4).bypassAll(Set(2,
      3))` keeps the edge `1 -> 4`. Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.
  entry #subgraph   the subgraph induced by a set of nodes: those nodes and the edges among them
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize` (`import dysasymptotics.linearSize`): a bounded choice of
      nodes rebuilds the whole map. An edge to a node outside the set is dropped, not rerouted.
  entry #flatMap   substitutes a graph for every node, as a `Digraph`
      For each edge `a -> b`, every node of `lambda(a)` points at every node of `lambda(b)`, and
      the nodes of `lambda(a)` keep their own edges within it. The result may hold a cycle, so
      it is a `Digraph`.
  entry #reachable   everything a node reaches, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
      The transitive dependencies of a node, as a set, by an iterative search with a visited
      set, so a chain of a hundred thousand nodes neither overflows the stack nor takes more
      than its reach. `Dag.Error(NodeMissing)` is raised only for a node the graph lacks.
  entry #descendants   the subgraph a node reaches, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
      `subgraph(reachable(node))`, with no acknowledgement needed: the induced subgraph of
      everything beneath the node.
  entry #ancestors   the subgraph that reaches a node, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
      Everything which depends on the node, transitively, with the edges among them. Needs
      `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`, since it walks the transpose.
  entry #lineage   the subgraph of everything a node reaches and everything that reaches it
      `ancestors` and `descendants` together, induced. Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan` and
      raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node.
  entry #closure   every implied edge made explicit: an edge wherever a path exists
      The transitive closure, through the frozen form's reachability matrix, in O(e·n/64)
      against the sum of all reaches that set unions would cost. `closure.reduction` is the
      `reduction`, and `reduction.closure` the `closure`.
  entry #reduction   the minimal graph with the same reachability: every edge a longer path implies, removed
      The transitive reduction, the form a dependency graph is usually drawn in. Every edge
      implied by a path of any length is removed, not only those a path of two edges implies,
      and only edges of the graph are kept. Computed on the frozen form by Aho, Garey and
      Ullman's method over its bit matrix.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Dag(1 -> 2, 2 -> 3, 3 -> 4, 1 -> 4).reduction.edges   // Set((1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4))
      ```
  entry #layered   the graph arranged in layers for drawing, as a `Layering`
      Each node is assigned to a layer, dependencies above dependents, and each layer ordered to
      reduce the crossings between the links joining it to its neighbours — Sugiyama's layered
      approach, as GraphViz's `dot` draws it. The `Ranking` in scope decides the layer: the
      companion default, `Ranking.LongestPath`, places every node one layer below its deepest
      dependency; `import rankings.balancedRanking` pulls each node with dependants down to the
      layer above its shallowest dependant. `dendrology`'s `LayeredDagDiagram` renders the
      result.
  entry #traversal   a value for every node, computed from the values of its dependencies
      `dag.traversal[result]((dependencyValues, node) => …)` visits the nodes in topological
      order, so each lambda call sees the already-computed values of the node's successors as a
      `Set`, and answers a `Map` from every node to its value. This is the shape of most work
      over a dependency graph — a build's costs, a propagated constraint, an accumulated
      transitive set — without hand-written recursion or visited-set bookkeeping.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val dag = Dag(8 -> Set(4, 6), 6 -> Set(3, 2), 4 -> Set(2))
      dag.traversal[Int]((below, node) => below.total + 1)   // one more than the sum beneath
      ```

entry .Digraph   a directed graph which may contain a cycle, as a persistent value
  detail
      A `Digraph[node]` is a finite directed graph, as an insertion-ordered map from each node
      to the set of nodes it points at — its dependencies, under the dependency reading. Every
      edge target is a node, so the constructors close the node set and a dangling edge is
      unrepresentable. Nothing about a `Digraph` presumes acyclicity: it is what the unchecked
      edits of a `Dag` (`+`, `++`, `map`, `flatMap`) answer, what `explore` materialises from a
      successor function, and what a graph is before `acyclic` has checked it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val graph = Digraph(1 -> 2, 2 -> 3, 3 -> 1)
      graph.cycle              // List(1, 2, 3, 1): a closed walk along edges
      graph.reachable(1)       // Set(1, 2, 3): total on a cyclic graph
      Digraph(1 -> 2).acyclic  // a Dag[Int]
      ```

      Because a cycle is possible, a `Digraph` has no `linearized`, `reduction`, `traversal` or
      `layered`: `acyclic` checks the graph once, in O(n + e), and answers the `Dag` which has
      them, raising `Dag.Error` (`Reason.Cyclic`) otherwise. Everything else a `Dag` offers — the
      node and edge queries, the editing methods, reachability and slicing, `closure` and
      `invert` — a `Digraph` offers too, with `reachable`, `closure` and the slices total on a
      cyclic graph (a visited set, not recursion). As a collection it is its nodes in the order
      they were given.

      Only the forward direction is stored, so `-`, `bypass`, `bypassAll`, `ancestors`,
      `lineage` and the `Bidirectional` instance take a `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`
      (`import dysasymptotics.linearScan`), and `subgraph` a `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize`. Two
      `Digraph`s are equal when their adjacency is.
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  refer acyclicity.explore
  entry .apply   a `Digraph` from edges, from nodes with their successors, from a node set and a function, or from a `Map`
      The same four shapes as `Dag.apply`, with no cycle check and no error: `Digraph(a -> b,
      …)` from edges (both ends become nodes, in order of first appearance), `Digraph(a ->
      Set(b, c), …)` from each node with its successors (a successor with no entry becomes a
      node), `Digraph(nodes)(node => successors)` from a node set and a function, and
      `Digraph(adjacency)` from a `Map[node, Set[node]]`, closed over every target.
  entry .nodal   the `Nodal` instance: a `Digraph`'s nodes and each node's successors
      In implicit scope through the companion; `has` is a map lookup.
  entry .bidirectional   the `Bidirectional` instance, gated on `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`
      Predecessors come from a transpose computed once per value by scanning every edge, so the
      instance needs `import dysasymptotics.linearScan` to resolve.
  entry .traversable   a `Digraph` as a collection of its nodes, in the order they were given
  entry .inclusive   `graph.has(node)`: whether the node is in the graph
  entry .countable   `graph.size` and `graph.nil`
  entry .mappable   `graph.map(lambda)`: renames the nodes, uniting the edges of any two which merge
  entry #nodes   every node, as a set
  entry #successors   the nodes a node points at, or the empty set for an absent node
  entry #edges   every edge as a `(from, to)` pair
  entry #sources   the nodes with no successors: those depending on nothing
  entry #sinks   the nodes with no predecessors: those nothing depends on
      One pass over the edges; no acknowledgement is needed, as the question is whole-graph.
  entry #invert   the same graph with every edge reversed
  entry #digraph   this graph itself
      So that code written against the `Dag`/`Digraph` surface can ask either for its `Digraph`.
  entry #including   the graph with a node added, with no edges, if it was absent
  entry #add   the graph with the edge `from -> to` added, unchecked
      A new target becomes a node. `+` takes the edge as a pair.
  entry #+   `graph + (from -> to)`: the edge added
  entry #++   `graph ++ other`: the union of two graphs' nodes and edges
  entry #remove   the graph with the edge `from -> to` removed, both nodes kept
  entry #-   `graph - node`: the node and every edge at either end of it dropped
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.
  entry #bypass   the node dropped, with each of its predecessors rerouted to each of its successors
      Every path through the node survives. Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.
  entry #bypassAll   several nodes bypassed one after another
      A `Set` of nodes, not a predicate; connectivity through a run of dropped nodes is
      preserved. Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.
  entry #subgraph   the subgraph induced by a set of nodes: those nodes and the edges among them
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize`.
  entry #reachable   everything a node reaches, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
      Total on a cyclic graph: `Digraph(1 -> 2, 2 -> 1).reachable(1)` is `Set(1, 2)`.
  entry #descendants   the subgraph a node reaches, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
  entry #ancestors   the subgraph that reaches a node, itself included; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`.
  entry #lineage   the subgraph of everything a node reaches and everything that reaches it
      Needs `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`; raises `Dag.Error` for an absent node.
  entry #cycle   a cycle, if the graph has one: nodes each pointing at the next, ending where it began
      `Unset` for an acyclic graph; otherwise a `List` whose first and last nodes are the same
      and whose every consecutive pair is an edge. Found by a depth-first search which also
      catches a cycle closed through a node an earlier branch had already finished.
  entry #acyclic   the graph as a `Dag`, after the one check; raises `Dag.Error` if there is a cycle
      O(n + e), once: the `Dag` it answers never checks again.

entry .Topology   a graph edited in place which keeps its topological order live, and, frozen, the form for querying
  detail
      A `Topology[node]` is a directed acyclic graph edited in place through an exclusively
      owned handle. Its name is for what it uniquely maintains: a topological order of the
      nodes, kept valid through every `add` by Pearce and Kelly's algorithm, which touches only
      the nodes between the two ends of a new edge and **refuses** an edge which would close a
      cycle, leaving the graph unchanged. So it is acyclic by invariant, and `linearized` is a
      read of the order.

      Mutability is the capture set, as for an `Array`: a `Topology[node]^` is editable, and
      `Topology.freeze` consumes it to give a `Topology[node]^{}`, on which `add`, `remove` and
      `bypass` are not callable and nothing can ever change. The frozen form is the one for
      querying — it carries the `Nodal`, `Bidirectional`, `Topological`, `Reachable` and
      `Invertible` instances — and keeps both directions of adjacency in dense arrays, with the
      sources and sinks maintained, so `predecessors` needs no `LinearScan` acknowledgement. Its
      reachability matrix, built on first use in O(e·n/64), answers `reaches` in O(1) and serves
      `closure` and `reduction`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An empty topology, exclusively owned
        code
            val topology: Topology[Text]^ = Topology()
            topology.linearized          // List()
      frame
        label An edge: both nodes join the order, dependency first
        code
            val topology: Topology[Text]^ = Topology()
            topology.add(t"app", t"lib")
            topology.linearized          // List(lib, app)
      frame
        label A dependency added below an existing node: the order is repaired, not rebuilt
        code
            val topology: Topology[Text]^ = Topology()
            topology.add(t"app", t"lib")
            topology.add(t"lib", t"core")
            topology.linearized          // List(core, lib, app)
      frame
        label An edge which would close a cycle is refused with Dag.Error, and nothing changes
        code
            val topology: Topology[Text]^ = Topology()
            topology.add(t"app", t"lib")
            topology.add(t"lib", t"core")
            safely(topology.add(t"core", t"app"))
            topology.linearized          // List(core, lib, app)
      frame
        label Freezing consumes the handle: the result can be queried but never edited
        code
            val topology: Topology[Text]^ = Topology()
            topology.add(t"app", t"lib")
            topology.add(t"lib", t"core")
            val frozen: Topology[Text]^{} = Topology.freeze(topology)
            frozen.reaches(t"app", t"core")   // true
            frozen.snapshot                   // the same graph as a Dag
      ##
      ```

      An editable `Topology` is for the graph that grows or changes incrementally — a build
      system discovering tasks, a scheduler accepting jobs: an edge costs well under a
      microsecond where a checked `Dag#add` costs the reach of its target. When the editing is
      done the handle is given up, to `Topology.freeze` or to `Dag(topology)`, so that the
      result can never be edited behind its back; `snapshot` takes a `Dag` without giving the
      handle up, and `Topology(frozen)` or `dag.thaw` makes a fresh editable copy. A bare
      `Topology[node]` in a signature means the editable `^` form.

      The matrix is cached against an edit revision: a frozen handle, which cannot be edited,
      keeps it, and an editable one which has been edited since recomputes it for each call —
      which is why `closure`, `reduction` and `reaches` are for the frozen form. The node keys
      are boxed and hashed through an open-addressing table, so generic keys cost what a
      `HashMap` of them would.
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  refer acyclicity.Topological
  refer acyclicity.Reachable
  entry .apply   a new empty topology, an editable copy of a frozen one, or an editable copy of a `Dag`
      `Topology[node]()` is an empty, editable `Topology[node]^`. `Topology(frozen)` copies a
      `Topology[node]^{}` into a fresh editable one — the way to edit a frozen graph, which
      itself cannot change — and `Topology(dag)` thaws a `Dag`, the same as `dag.thaw`. Both
      copies add the nodes in topological order, so no edge needs reordering.
  entry .freeze   consumes an editable topology and answers it frozen, as a `Topology[node]^{}`
      `Topology.freeze(topology)` is the only producer of a frozen topology from an editable
      one (`Dag#freeze` builds its own). The handle is **consumed**: every writer is statically
      retired, so the surviving reference drops its exclusivity without a copy, and the editing
      methods are no longer callable on it.
  entry .nodal   the `Nodal` instance of a frozen `Topology[node]^{}`
      Its nodes in topological order, each node's successors from its out-list, and `has` by a
      hash probe. The instances are for the frozen form only: an editable handle is not `Nodal`.
  entry .bidirectional   the `Bidirectional` instance of a frozen topology, with no acknowledgement
      Predecessors are read from the in-lists the topology keeps, so no `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`
      is needed, unlike a `Dag`.
  entry .topological   the marker which certifies a frozen topology holds no cycle
  entry .traversable   a frozen topology as a collection of its nodes, in topological order
  entry .inclusive   `frozen.has(node)`
  entry .countable   `frozen.size` and `frozen.nil`
  entry .grown   an `Int` array copied into a larger one; a helper of the implementation
      Not for direct use. The growth helpers live on the companion rather than the class so
      that the separation checker sees only their arguments.
  entry .grownLongs   a `Long` array copied into a larger one; a helper of the implementation
  entry .grownRefs   an `AnyRef` array copied into a larger one; a helper of the implementation
  entry .grownFlags   a `Boolean` array copied into a larger one; a helper of the implementation
  entry .hash   a key's hash with its high bits folded into the low ones; a helper of the implementation
  entry #size   how many nodes the topology currently has
  entry #edgeCount   how many edges the topology currently has
  entry #has   whether a node is in the topology, by a hash probe
  entry #add   adds a node, or adds the edge `from -> to`, raising `Dag.Error` if it would close a cycle
      On an editable handle only. `add(node)` adds a node at the end of the order, if absent,
      and answers its internal id; `add(from, to)` adds the edge (`from` depends on `to`), adding
      either node if absent. If `to` ranks below `from` already nothing moves; otherwise the
      dependants of `from` and the dependencies of `to` which lie between the two are reordered
      — and if that search meets `to` among `from`'s dependants, the edge would close a cycle:
      `Dag.Error` (`Reason.Cyclic`) is raised through the `Tactic[Dag.Error]` in scope and the
      graph is left exactly as it was. A duplicate edge is ignored.
  entry #remove   drops a node and every edge at either end of it
      On an editable handle only. Unknown nodes are ignored.
  entry #bypass   drops a node, rerouting each of its dependants to each of its dependencies
      On an editable handle only. No reordering can be needed, since every dependant already
      ranks above every dependency.
  entry #reachable   everything a node reaches, itself included; the empty set for an absent node
      A depth-first walk of the out-lists; unlike `Dag#reachable`, an absent node answers the
      empty set rather than raising.
  entry #reaches   whether one node reaches another, in O(1) once the reachability matrix exists
      `frozen.reaches(from, to)`: a single bit of the matrix, which is built on first use and
      kept on a frozen handle. `false` if either node is absent.
  entry #edges   every edge as a `(from, to)` pair
  entry #successors   the nodes a node points at, or the empty set for an absent node
  entry #predecessors   the nodes pointing at a node, from the in-lists kept for it
      No acknowledgement is needed: the topology stores both directions.
  entry #sources   the nodes with no successors, maintained as the graph changes
  entry #sinks   the nodes with no predecessors, maintained as the graph changes
  entry #linearized   the maintained topological order, dependencies first: a read, in O(n)
  entry #nodes   every node, as a set
  entry #closure   every implied edge made explicit, as a `Dag`, from the reachability matrix
      For a frozen handle. Each node's row of the matrix becomes its successor set.
  entry #reduction   the minimal graph with the same reachability, as a `Dag`, from the reachability matrix
      For a frozen handle. Aho, Garey and Ullman's method: visiting a node's successors by
      descending rank with a running union of their rows decides every edge in
      O(degree·n/64), so the reduction of a thousand-node graph takes a fraction of a
      millisecond.
  entry #invert   every edge reversed, as another frozen topology
      The reversed order is a valid order for the reversed edges, so the copy needs no
      reordering; `frozen.invert.linearized` is `frozen.linearized.reverse`.
  entry #snapshot   a persistent `Dag` of the current graph, without giving up the handle

entry .Nodal   the typeclass of a finite directed graph: a node set and each node's successors
  detail
      `graph is Nodal by node` says a value of type `graph` is a finite directed graph over
      `node`: it can enumerate its `nodes`, answer the `successors` of any node — the nodes it
      points at, which under the dependency reading used throughout Soundness (`a -> b` means
      `a` requires `b`) are its dependencies — and say whether it `has` a node, as a primitive
      rather than a scan, so a missing-node check is never O(n). Edge direction is fixed by
      contract, not by instance: a topological order lists a node after its successors.

      Instances are provided for `Dag`, `Digraph`, a frozen `Topology[node]^{}` and `Hasse`
      (each in its own companion), and here for any `Map[node, Set[node]]` and `Ledger[node,
      Set[node]]`, so a plain map of dependencies answers the package-level extensions —
      `nodes`, `edges`, `sources`, `sinks`, `cycle`, `acyclic`, `reachable`, `closure` and
      `invert` — with no conversion:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val deps = Map(1 -> Set(2), 2 -> Set[Int]())
      deps.reachable(1)       // Set(1, 2)
      deps.invert.edges       // Set((2, 1))
      deps.acyclic            // a Dag[Int]
      ```

      The methods return `murmuration.Iterator`s, the cheapest thing every representation can
      produce; the extensions turn them into `Set`s and `List`s. An instance of one's own which
      has a stdlib iterator wraps it with `Iterator.from(…)`. A successor *function*, `node =>
      Iterable[node]`, is deliberately not `Nodal` — it has no finite node set and no decidable
      `has` — and `explore` materialises one into a `Digraph`. A representation which also
      stores predecessors says so through `Bidirectional`, and one whose values can hold no
      cycle through `Topological`.
  refer acyclicity.Bidirectional
  refer acyclicity.Topological
  refer acyclicity.Reachable
  refer acyclicity.Invertible
  entry .map   the `Nodal` instance of any `Map[node, Set[node]]`
      In implicit scope through the typeclass's companion, since `Map` belongs to proscenium. A
      target absent from the key set is **not** a node: the instance reports the map as it is,
      and `Digraph(adjacency)` is the constructor which closes the node set over targets. The
      instance is subtype-parametric, so a refined map type matches too.
  entry .ledger   the `Nodal` instance of any `Ledger[node, Set[node]]`
      As `.map`, for a `Ledger`: its keys are the nodes, in their order, and a missing key has
      no successors.
  entry #nodes   every node, as an iterator
  entry #successors   the nodes a node points at, as an iterator; empty for an absent node
  entry #has   whether the graph has a node, without scanning its nodes

entry .Bidirectional   the typeclass of a graph which can answer a node's predecessors directly
  detail
      `graph is Bidirectional by node` says the graph can answer which nodes point *at* a node —
      its dependants, under the dependency reading — without scanning every edge. A frozen
      `Topology` and a `Hasse` store both directions and have ungated instances. A `Dag` or
      `Digraph` stores only the forward direction, and its instance is gated behind
      `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan`: locating one node's predecessors there is a transpose of every
      edge, a cost outside the question's scope, which `import dysasymptotics.linearScan` makes
      a program acknowledge. The package-level `predecessors` extension needs this instance.
  refer acyclicity.Nodal
  entry #predecessors   the nodes pointing at a node, as an iterator

entry .Topological   the marker typeclass of a graph type whose values can hold no cycle
    `graph is Topological` certifies that a value of the type admits a topological order — that
    it contains no directed cycle — **by construction**: `Dag`, a frozen `Topology[node]^{}` and
    `Hasse` each have an instance, since each can only be built through a check or an
    invariant. The instance is a marker, never the result of a runtime check on a general
    type: a `Digraph` becomes `Topological` only by `acyclic`, which answers a `Dag`. The
    extensions `linearized`, `reduction`, `traversal` and `layered` require it, as do the
    `dendrology` diagrams, so a cyclic graph has no such operations rather than operations
    which raise.

entry .Reachable   the typeclass of a graph with its own reachability search
  detail
      `graph is Reachable by node` answers everything reachable from a node, the node included,
      as a set — total on cyclic input, by a visited set rather than recursion, so it never
      raises on its own account; the `reachable` extension which calls it raises `Dag.Error`
      only for a node the graph does not have. The native instance is a frozen `Topology`'s,
      which walks its edge lists; any other `Nodal` graph is searched generically through
      `.generic`, which the extensions take as the default of their contextual parameter, so
      a `Map` or a `Hasse` needs no instance of its own.
  refer acyclicity.Nodal
  entry .topology   the `Reachable` instance of a frozen `Topology[node]^{}`
  entry .generic   a `Reachable` for any `Nodal` graph, by a depth-first search through its successors
      A method rather than a given: the `reachable` and `closure` extensions take it as the
      default of their `using` parameter, so that it is reached without a nested given search —
      which, for an opaque receiver such as `Map`, would see the alias dealiased and match
      nothing. Code summoning a `Reachable` for its own graph type can call it directly.
  entry #reachable   everything a node reaches, itself included

entry .Invertible   the typeclass of a graph which can reverse every edge, and the type it reverses into
  detail
      `graph is Invertible by node to result` answers the graph with every edge reversed, as a
      value of the type best placed to hold it: a `Dag` inverts to a `Dag`, since reversing every
      edge preserves acyclicity; a `Hasse` to a `Hasse` in O(1), by swapping the two directions it
      stores; a frozen `Topology` to another frozen `Topology`; and anything else `Nodal` to a
      `Digraph`, by a fold over its edges through `.generic`, which the `invert` extension takes
      as the default of its contextual parameter.
  refer acyclicity.Nodal
  entry .digraph   `Digraph` inverts to `Digraph`
  entry .dag   `Dag` inverts to `Dag`: reversing every edge preserves acyclicity
  entry .topology   a frozen `Topology[node]^{}` inverts to another
  entry .hasse   `Hasse` inverts to `Hasse`, in O(1), by swapping its two stored directions
  entry .generic   an `Invertible` for any `Nodal` graph, answering a `Digraph`
      A method, not a given, for the same reason as `Reachable.generic`: the extension takes it
      as its default, avoiding a nested given search which an opaque receiver would defeat.
  entry #invert   the graph with every edge reversed, as the instance's `Result`

entry .Hasse   the Hasse diagram of a partial order: each element's immediate covers, with implied comparisons dropped
  detail
      `Hasse(elements)(lessOrEqual)` takes a set and an ordering relation (`lessOrEqual(a, b)`
      meaning `a ≤ b`) and computes the covering relation: for each element, the elements
      immediately above it (`parents`) and immediately below it (`children`), with every
      comparison a path already implies left out. The divisors of 12, ordered by divisibility:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val divisors = Hasse(Set(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12))((a, b) => b%a == 0)

      divisors.parents(2)      // Set(4, 6): the covers of 2
      divisors.children(6)     // Set(2, 3)
      divisors.children(12)    // Set(4, 6), not 2: a transitive edge is not a cover
      divisors.maxima          // Set(12)
      divisors.minima          // Set(1)
      ```

      Elements are inserted one at a time; each insertion finds its immediate parents by
      descending from the current maxima and its immediate children by ascending from the
      current minima, pruning whole subtrees as soon as a comparison rules them out, and every
      comparison is memoised, so far fewer than all ordered pairs are compared. Two elements
      equivalent under the relation (each ≤ the other) are merged: the later one becomes an
      alias of the first, and queries on either answer for both.

      A `Hasse` is itself a graph, each element pointing at the elements it covers, so it is
      `Nodal`, `Bidirectional` (both directions are stored) and `Topological` (a partial order
      has no cycle): `divisors.linearized` runs from the bottom to the top, its `sources` are
      its minima and its `sinks` its maxima, and `dendrology`'s diagrams draw it directly.
      `dag` is the same relation as a `Dag`, and `Poset#dag` builds one through a
      `PartiallyOrdered` instance.
  refer acyclicity.Poset
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  entry .apply   the Hasse diagram of a set under a `lessOrEqual` relation
      `Hasse(elements)((a, b) => a ≤ b)`. The relation must be a partial order (reflexive,
      antisymmetric up to the merging of equivalents, transitive) for the result to be a
      covering relation; it is called at most once per ordered pair.
  entry .nodal   the `Nodal` instance: a `Hasse`'s elements, each pointing at the elements it covers
  entry .bidirectional   the `Bidirectional` instance: an element's predecessors are its covers, read directly
      Both directions are stored, so no `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan` is needed.
  entry .topological   the marker which certifies a `Hasse` holds no cycle: a partial order cannot
  entry #parents   the elements immediately above an element: its covers
  entry #children   the elements immediately below an element: those it covers
  entry #elements   every element, as a set
  entry #maxima   the elements nothing is above
  entry #minima   the elements nothing is below
  entry #dag   the covering relation as a `Dag`, each element pointing at those it covers
      No check is needed: a partial order's covering relation is acyclic. `hasse.dag.edges` is
      `hasse.edges`.
  entry #invert   the same relation read upward: each element pointing at those covering it
      O(1): the two stored directions are swapped. `invert.children(x)` is `parents(x)`.
  entry #bottom   a new element attached below every current minimum, in one comparison-free sweep
      For a value known by construction to be less than everything — `Nothing` among types —
      so that it need not be compared against each element. It becomes the sole minimum, and
      each former minimum now covers it.
  entry #top   a new element attached above every current maximum, in one comparison-free sweep
      The counterpart of `#bottom`: `Any` among types. It becomes the sole maximum.

entry .PartiallyOrdered   the typeclass of a type with a partial order: `compare(left, right)` is `left ≤ right`
  detail
      `element is PartiallyOrdered` supplies `compare(left, right): Boolean`, read as `left ≤
      right`, for `Poset`. The relation should be reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive; it
      need not relate every pair. A given is written as a lambda:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Int is PartiallyOrdered = (a, b) => b%a == 0   // divisibility
      Poset(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12).dag.successors(2)           // Set(4, 6)
      ```
  refer acyclicity.Poset

entry .Poset   a set of elements with a `PartiallyOrdered` instance, and the `Dag` of its covering relation
  detail
      `Poset(elements*)` holds a set of elements whose type is `PartiallyOrdered`, and `dag` is
      the Hasse diagram of the order as a `Dag`, each element pointing at the elements
      immediately **above** it (its covers), built through `Hasse` with the order's `compare`,
      which prunes comparisons and needs no reduction afterwards.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Int is PartiallyOrdered = (a, b) => b%a == 0
      Poset(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12).dag.successors(2)   // Set(4, 6)
      ```

      Note the direction: `Poset#dag` points upward (at covers), where `Hasse#dag` points
      downward (at the covered); `Hasse` with the same relation is the richer value, with both
      directions, maxima and minima.
  refer acyclicity.Hasse
  refer acyclicity.PartiallyOrdered
  entry .apply   a `Poset` of the given elements
  entry #elements   the elements, as a set
  entry #dag   the covering relation as a `Dag`, each element pointing at those immediately above it

entry .Layering   a graph arranged in layers for drawing, dependencies above dependents, with crossings reduced
  detail
      The result of `Dag#layered` (and the `layered` extension on any `Topological` graph).
      Every node is assigned to a layer — by `Ranking.LongestPath`, one layer below its
      deepest dependency, so the sources share layer 0; or, with `import
      rankings.balancedRanking`, pulled down to the layer above its shallowest dependant — and
      an edge spanning more than one layer passes through a `Layering.Vertex.Virtual` in each
      layer between, so every link in the result joins adjacent layers. The vertices of each
      layer are then ordered to reduce the crossings between the links joining it to its
      neighbours, by alternating weighted-median sweeps and adjacent transpositions, up to
      twenty-four sweeps or until the drawing is planar — Sugiyama's method, as GraphViz's
      `dot` draws it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val dag = Dag(8 -> Set(4, 6), 6 -> Set(3, 2), 4 -> Set(2), 3 -> Set(), 2 -> Set())
      val layering = dag.layered

      layering.rank(2)          // 0: no dependencies, so the first layer
      layering.rank(8)          // 2: two layers down, by the longest path
      layering.layers(1)        // the vertices of the second layer, in drawn order
      layering.links(0)         // the (upper, lower) positions joined between layers 0 and 1
      layering.crossings        // 0: no link crosses another
      ```

      `dendrology`'s `LayeredDagDiagram` assigns columns to a layering and renders it; a
      different drawing takes `layers` and `links` directly.
  refer acyclicity.Ranking
  refer acyclicity.rankings
  entry .Vertex   a slot in a layer: a node of the graph, or the point where a longer edge passes through
      An enum: `Real(value)` is a node of the graph, and `Virtual(source, target)` is where the
      edge from `source` to `target`, spanning several layers, passes through this one. A
      drawing gives a virtual vertex a column and draws the edge through it.
  entry #layers   the layers, top down, each a list of `Layering.Vertex` in drawn order
  entry #links   `links(i)` joins layer `i` to layer `i + 1`, as pairs of positions within each
      Every link joins adjacent layers: an edge spanning more is broken at its virtual vertices.
  entry #rank   each node's layer, as a `Map`
  entry #position   each node's position within its layer, as a `Map`
  entry #crossings   how many pairs of links cross, over the whole layering

entry .Ranking   how `layered` assigns each node to a layer: `LongestPath` or `Balanced`
  detail
      An enum, taken as a contextual value by `Dag#layered`, the `layered` extension and
      `dendrology`'s `LayeredDagDiagram`. `LongestPath` places every node one layer below its
      deepest dependency, so every node depending on nothing shares layer 0. `Balanced` starts
      from that and then pulls every node which has dependants down to the layer above its
      shallowest dependant, which shortens the long edge from a source that only feeds
      something deep. The companion's `default` is `LongestPath`; `import
      rankings.balancedRanking` outranks it.
  refer acyclicity.rankings
  refer acyclicity.Layering
  entry .default   `Ranking.LongestPath`, the given which applies when none is imported

entry .rankings   the two layer-assignment policies for `layered`, as givens to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` resolves `Ranking.LongestPath` from the `Ranking` companion; importing
      one of these by name selects a policy explicitly, and `balancedRanking` is the only way to
      select `Balanced`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import rankings.balancedRanking

      val deep = Dag(1 -> Set(), 5 -> Set(), 2 -> Set(1), 3 -> Set(2, 5))
      deep.layered.rank(5)      // 1: 5 feeds only 3, two layers down, so it sinks to the layer above
      ```

      Without the import `deep.layered.rank(5)` is 0.
  entry .longestPathRanking   `Ranking.LongestPath`: every node one layer below its deepest dependency
  entry .balancedRanking   `Ranking.Balanced`: every node with dependants pulled down to the layer above its shallowest dependant

entry .Dot   a GraphViz DOT document as a value, built from identifiers, edges and attributes, and serialized to text
  detail
      `Dot` is an enum of two cases, `Graph` (undirected) and `Digraph` (directed), each holding
      an optional name, whether it is `strict` (no duplicate edges), and its `Dot.Statement`s.
      The companion's `digraph`, `strictDigraph`, `graph`, `strictGraph` and `subgraph` build
      them, and the statements are built from `Name[Dot.Id]` identifiers with the extension
      operators `-->` (a directed edge), `--` (an undirected edge), `:=` (an assignment) and
      `id(key -> value, …)` (a node with attributes). `serialize` writes the DOT text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val a = Name[Dot.Id](t"a")
      val b = Name[Dot.Id](t"b")

      Dot.digraph(Name[Dot.Id](t"g"), a --> b, a(t"color" -> t"red")).serialize
      // "\ndigraph g {\n  \"a\" -> \"b\"\n  \"a\" [ color=\"red\" ]\n}"

      Dot.digraph(Dot.subgraph(a --> b)).serialize
      // "\ndigraph {\n  subgraph {\n    \"a\" -> \"b\"\n  }\n}"
      ```

      Every identifier is emitted double-quoted, so the `Dot.Identifier` rule is permissive:
      any non-empty text with no double-quote and no newline. `Name[Dot.Id](text)` raises
      `Name.Error` for anything else (needing a `Tactic[Name.Error]`), and the `n"…"` literal
      is checked as the code compiles. A `Dag[Text]` renders through `dag.dot`, which makes a
      `Dot.Digraph` with an edge per dependency.
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  entry .digraph   a directed graph, named or anonymous, of the given statements
      `Dot.digraph(id, statements*)` or `Dot.digraph(statements*)`; not strict.
  entry .strictDigraph   a named directed graph marked `strict`: duplicate edges are merged
  entry .graph   a named undirected graph of the given statements
  entry .strictGraph   a named undirected graph marked `strict`
  entry .subgraph   a `subgraph { … }` statement, named or anonymous, nested in a graph or as an edge target
      `Dot.subgraph(id, statements*)` or `Dot.subgraph(statements*)`. An edge may end at a
      subgraph: `a --> Dot.subgraph(b, c)`.
  entry .--   `a -- b`: an undirected edge statement, from an identifier to an identifier or a subgraph
  entry .-->   `a --> b`: a directed edge statement, from an identifier to an identifier or a subgraph
  entry .:=   `a := b`: an assignment statement, emitted as `"a" = "b"`
  entry .apply   `id(key -> value, …)`: a node statement declaring an identifier with attributes
      An extension on `Name[Dot.Id]`: `a(t"color" -> t"red")` is emitted as `"a" [ color="red" ]`.
      With no attributes it declares the bare node.
  entry .Target   the right-hand side of an edge: directed or not, its destination, and an optional chained target
    detail
        A case class with `directed`, `dest` (an identifier or a `Statement.Subgraph`) and `link`,
        a further `Target` for a chain such as `a -> b -> c`. The `-->` and `--` operators build
        one; it is rarely written by hand.
    entry #directed   whether the edge is drawn with `->` rather than `--`
    entry #dest   the destination: a `Name[Dot.Id]` or a `Statement.Subgraph`
    entry #link   a further target chained after this one, if any
  entry .Property   one `key="value"` attribute of a node
    entry #key   the attribute's name
    entry #value   the attribute's value, emitted double-quoted
  entry .Statement   one statement of a graph: a node, an edge, an assignment or a subgraph
      An enum: `Node(id, attrs*)`, `Edge(id, rhs: Target, attrs*)`, `Assignment(id, id2)` and
      `Subgraph(id: Option[Name[Dot.Id]], statements*)`. The companion's constructors and the
      operators on `Name[Dot.Id]` make them; a `Subgraph` may also stand as an edge's target.
  entry .serialize   `Dot.serialize(tokens)`: lays out a token list as indented DOT text
      The low-level writer the instance method uses: braces open and close indented blocks,
      `;` ends a line, `[` and `]` bracket attributes, and other tokens are space-separated.
  entry .Id   the naming plane of DOT identifiers: `Name[Dot.Id]`
    detail
        A sealed marker type. A `Name[Dot.Id]` names a node, an edge endpoint, an assignment
        side or a (sub)graph alike, and is held to `Dot.Identifier` by the given `nominative` in
        this object: `Name[Dot.Id](text)` checks at runtime (raising `Name.Error`) and `n"…"`
        as the code compiles.
    entry .nominative   the given which holds a `Name[Dot.Id]` to the `Dot.Identifier` rule
  entry .Identifier   the rule a DOT identifier must satisfy: non-empty, with no double-quote and no newline
      A `nomenclature` rule. Identifiers are always emitted double-quoted, so nothing else needs
      escaping; its failure message reads _must be a valid DOT identifier_.
  entry #serialize   the graph as DOT text, indented, beginning with a newline
      `Dag(t"a" -> t"b").dot.serialize` is `"\ndigraph {\n  \"a\" -> \"b\"\n}"`.
  entry #add   the graph with further statements appended

entry .explore   `start.explore(dependencies)`: materialises a successor function into a `Digraph` by exploring from a node
    An extension on any value. A function `node => Iterable[node]` has no finite node set of its
    own, so it is not `Nodal`; `explore` walks outward from `start`, calling the function once
    per node reached, and answers the graph of everything found. The function may lead back on
    itself, so the result is a `Digraph`, and `acyclic` checks it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    12.explore(n => (1 until n).filter(n%_ == 0)).acyclic   // the divisibility graph beneath 12
    ```

entry .nodes   every node of any `Nodal` graph, as a set
    The generic extension, for any `graph is Nodal by node`: a `Map[node, Set[node]]`, a
    `Ledger`, a `Hasse`, a frozen `Topology` or either graph type (which offer the same names as
    methods, taking precedence).

entry .successors   the nodes a node of any `Nodal` graph points at: its dependencies, as a set

entry .edges   every edge of any `Nodal` graph, as `(from, to)` pairs

entry .sources   the nodes of any `Nodal` graph with no successors: those depending on nothing
    Whole-graph in scope, so no acknowledgement is needed on a forward-only representation.

entry .sinks   the nodes of any `Nodal` graph with no predecessors: those nothing depends on
    One pass over every edge, so no `Bidirectional` instance and no acknowledgement is needed:
    the question is about the whole graph, not one node.

entry .predecessors   the nodes pointing at a node, for any `Bidirectional` graph
    Needs a `graph is Bidirectional by node`: ungated for a frozen `Topology` or a `Hasse`, and
    gated on `Dysasymptotic.LinearScan` (`import dysasymptotics.linearScan`) for a `Dag` or a
    `Digraph`, which must transpose every edge to answer.

entry .cycle   a cycle in any `Nodal` graph, if it has one: nodes each pointing at the next, ending where it began
    `Unset` for an acyclic graph. Total on any graph, by a depth-first search.

entry .acyclic   any `Nodal` graph as a `Dag`, after the one check; raises `Dag.Error` if there is a cycle
    O(n + e), once; the node set is closed over every edge target.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    Map(1 -> Set(2), 2 -> Set[Int]()).acyclic.linearized   // List(2, 1)
    ```

entry .digraph   any `Nodal` graph copied into a `Digraph`, its node set closed over every edge target

entry .reachable   everything a node of any `Nodal` graph reaches, itself included
    Raises `Dag.Error` (`Reason.NodeMissing`) only for a node the graph does not have, by the
    instance's `has`; the search itself is total on a cyclic graph. A graph with its own
    `Reachable` instance (a frozen `Topology`) is searched natively, any other through
    `Reachable.generic`.

entry .closure   every implied edge of any `Nodal` graph made explicit, as a `Digraph`
    One reachability search per node, through the graph's `Reachable` instance or the generic
    search; a node does not point at itself in the result. `Dag#closure` and a frozen
    `Topology`'s are the faster forms, through the bit matrix.

entry .invert   any `Nodal` graph with every edge reversed, as the type its `Invertible` instance names
    A `Dag` to a `Dag`, a `Hasse` to a `Hasse`, a frozen `Topology` to another, and anything
    else — a `Map`, a `Ledger` — to a `Digraph` through `Invertible.generic`.

entry .linearized   the nodes of any `Topological` graph in topological order: every node after its successors
    For a `Dag`, a frozen `Topology` or a `Hasse`; a `Digraph` has no such operation until
    `acyclic` has converted it. Between unrelated nodes the order follows the instance's
    `nodes`, so an insertion-ordered representation gives a stable one.

entry .reduction   the minimal graph with the same reachability as any `Topological` graph, as a `Dag`
    Through a frozen `Topology`'s bit matrix, built once for the call.

entry .traversal   a value for every node of any `Topological` graph, from the values of its successors
    `graph.traversal[result]((successorValues, node) => …)`: the nodes are visited in
    topological order, so each call sees the values already computed for the node's
    successors, and the result is a `Map` from every node to its value.

entry .layered   any `Topological` graph arranged in crossing-minimised layers, as a `Layering`
    Takes the `Ranking` in scope (`Ranking.LongestPath` by default; `import
    rankings.balancedRanking` to change it). A graph other than a `Dag` is converted once,
    unchecked, since `Topological` certifies it has no cycle.
